  "historical_output_text": " The jet stream is a strong wind high up in the atmosphere that usually flows from west to east across the mid-latitudes of the Northern Hemisphere. It's like a river of air that helps move weather systems along. When the jet stream is in its normal pattern, it helps keep cold Arctic air to the north and warmer air to the south. \n\nHowever, sometimes the jet stream can get wavy or \"bent\" into a more north-south orientation. When this happens, it can allow cold Arctic air to dip much farther south than normal, and warm air to push farther north. This can lead to unusually cold or warm temperatures",
